 ### [Separable Component-Wise Convolution](7446-separable-component-wise-convolution.md)
-Separable component-wise convolution is a computationally efficient method for applying 3D Gaussian smoothing to volumes represented as decomposed low-rank tensors. It solves the problem of high computational cost and memory usage associated with naively convolving a 3D kernel on a reconstructed tensor volume. Unlike traditional separated kernel convolution which only exploits kernel separability, this method distributes the convolution across individual tensor components by leveraging the separability of the input signal itself, thereby preserving the space compression advantage of the low-rank representation.
+Separable component-wise convolution is a computationally efficient method for applying 3D Gaussian smoothing to volumes represented as decomposed low-rank tensors. It solves the problem of high computational cost and memory usage associated with naively convolving a 3D kernel on a reconstructed full tensor volume. Unlike traditional separated kernel convolution which only exploits kernel separability, this method distributes the convolution across individual vector and matrix components of the decomposed tensor, leveraging the input signal's inherent separability to maintain space compression and reduce time complexity.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(\text{size}^3)$ to generate the kernel. Convolution using this kernel is $O(\text{size}^3 \cdot N)$ for $N$ voxels.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(\text{size}^3)$ to store the kernel.
-- **Strengths:** Provides smooth, isotropic smoothing. The zero-center modification prevents the kernel from reinforcing the center value directly, which can be useful in specific gradient-based or difference-of-Gaussians contexts.
-- **Limitations:** The explicit zeroing of the center element breaks the standard property of a Gaussian kernel where the center is the maximum weight. This may lead to unexpected artifacts if the user assumes a standard Gaussian blur. Not given in the sources: performance on GPU vs CPU.
-- **When to use:** When smoothing is required but the direct contribution of the center voxel must be nullified.
-- **When not to use:** For standard image or volume blurring where preserving the center intensity is desired.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ where $N$ is the kernel size `size`, as every element must be computed.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N^3)$ to store the kernel.
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; provides smooth, isotropic smoothing.
+- **Limitations:** The center-zero constraint makes it unsuitable for standard blurring where the center should retain maximum weight; it is better suited for edge detection or specific gradient-based operations. Small kernel sizes (e.g., 3x3x3) may not capture long-range dependencies.
+- **When to use:** When a smoothing kernel with a null center is required for specific volume rendering or voxelization steps.
+- **When not to use:** For standard image/volume blurring where the center should be the peak.
 
 ## Background
-Origin: The method is documented in the unofficial repository [DanJbk/Plenoxels](https://github.com/DanJbk/Plenoxels) [S1, unofficial]. It appears to be a utility function for generating smoothing kernels within the context of neural radiance fields or voxel-based rendering pipelines, specifically adapted for alpha compositing or light source generation.
+Originated in the context of voxel-based neural rendering and volume processing, specifically within implementations like Plenoxels [S1, unofficial] and TensoRF variants [S2, unofficial]. The center-zero variant is a specific modification to the standard Gaussian kernel, likely to prevent self-interference or to act as a difference-of-Gaussians-like operator in certain pipelines.
 
